        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:43:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118820,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 53,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "<p>Bone marrow biopsy sections usually show hypercellularity with infiltration of a monotonous blast population. Blasts may have azurophilic granules and Auer rods. Maturing granulocytic-lineage cells, including promyelocytes, myelocytes, and mature neutrophils constitute &ge;&nbsp;10% of bone marrow cells. Variable degrees of mainly neutrophilic dysplasia may be present. Monocytes should not be increased and comprise &lt;&nbsp;20% of bone marrow cells.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>The blasts usually show intermediate to strong expression of myeloperoxidase and two or more myeloid-associated antigens, such as CD13, CD33, and CD117. Blasts are often positive for CD34 and HLA-DR. They usually express markers associated with granulocytic maturation, including CD11b, CD15, and CD65. The expression of monocytic markers (e.g. CD11c, CD14, CD36, and CD64) is rare. CD7 and CD56 are occasionally expressed. Expression of CD2, CD5, CD19, c/sCD22, and cCD79a is uncommon.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Cytochemistry</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>Blasts are positive for myeloperoxidase and/or Sudan Black B. They are negative or show limited positivity for naphthol AS-D CAE.</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:43:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118821,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 53,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>See above.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:43:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118822,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 53,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Approximately two&nbsp;thirds of cases have a normal karyotype. The rest have either single trisomies (e.g. trisomy&nbsp;8, 11, 13, or&nbsp;21) or rare structural abnormalities {15527902}. Frequently mutated genes include <em>ASXL1</em> (~40%), <em>RUNX1</em> (~30%), <em>STAG2</em> (~30%), <em>IDH2</em> (~25%), and <em>DNMT3A</em> (~20%); less commonly mutated genes include <em>IDH1</em>, <em>TET2</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, <em>SRSF2</em>, <em>U2AF1</em>, <em>SF3B1</em>, and <em>ZRSR2</em>. <em>FLT3</em> internal tandem duplication (ITD) and tyrosine kinase domain (TKD) mutations are present in 5&ndash;10% and 3% of cases, respectively {27285584}. It should be noted that some of the aforementioned mutations now qualify for myelodysplasia-related AML (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Acute myeloid leukaemia, myelodysplasia-related</em>).</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:43:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118823,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 53,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> &ge;&nbsp;20% blasts in bone marrow and/or blood, with cytochemical evidence of myeloid differentiation and morphological features of granulocytic maturation in &ge;&nbsp;10% of bone marrow cells; positive for at least two myeloid-associated markers (e.g. myeloperoxidase, CD13, CD33, CD117); monocytic-lineage cells constitute &lt;&nbsp;20% of bone marrow cells; not fulfilling criteria for AML types with defined genetic alterations; not fulfilling diagnostic criteria for myeloid neoplasm post cytotoxic therapy.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:43:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
